                               CONTROLS\P\P\PIT IS IN THE CONTRACTORS RESPONSIBILITY TO IMPLEMENT THE \PEROSION AND TURBIDITY CONTROLS AS SHOWN ON THE EROSION AND \PTURBIDITY CONTROL PLAN. IT IS ALSO THE CONTRACTORS \PRESPONSIBILITY  TO ENSURE THESE 
  3
CONTROLS ARE PROPERLY \PINSTALLED, MAINTAINED AND FUNCTIONING PROPERLY TO PREVENT \PTURBID OR POLLUTED WATER FROM LEAVING THE PROJECT SITE. THE \PCONTRACTOR WILL ADJUST THE EROSION AND TURBIDITY CONTROLS \PSHOWN ON THE EROSION AND TURBIDITY CONTROL P
  3
LAN AND ADD \PADDITIONAL CONTROL MEASURES. AS REQUIRED TO ENSURE THE SITE \PMEETS ALL FEDERAL, STATE, AND LOCAL EROSION AND TURBIDITY \PCONTROL REQUIREMENTS. THE FOLLOWING BEST MANAGEMENT \PPRACTICES WILL BE IMPLEMENTED BY THE CONTRACTOR AS REQUIRED 
  3
\PBY THE EROSION AND TURBIDITY CONTROL PLAN AND AS REQUIRED TO \PMEET THE EROSION AND TURBIDITY REQUIREMENTS IMPOSED ON THE \PPROJECT SITE BY THE REGULATORY AGENCIES.\P\PEROSION AND SEDIMENT CONTROLS\PSTABILIZATION PRACTICES\P\P1. STRAW BALE BARRIER:
  3
 STRAW BALE BARRIERS CAN BE USED \P   BELOW DISTURBED AREAS SUBJECT TO SHEET AND RILL EROSION \P   WITH THE FOLLOWING LIMITATIONS:\P\P  A. WHERE THE MAXIMUM SLOPE BEHIND THE BARRIER IS 33  \P     PERCENT.\P  B. IN MINOR SWALES OR DITCH LINES WHERE TH
  3
E MAXIMUM \P     CONTRIBUTING DRAINAGE AREA IS NO GREATER THAN 2 \P     ACRES.\P  C. WHERE EFFECTIVENESS IS REQUIRED FOR LESS THAN 3 \P     MONTHS\P  D. EVERY EFFORT SHOULD BE MADE TO LIMIT THE USE OF  \P     STRAW BALE BARRIERS CONSTRUCTED IN LIVE S
  3
TREAMS\P     OR IN SWALES WHERE THERE IS THE POSSIBILITY OF A \P     WASHOUT. IF NECESSARY, MEASURES SHALL BE TAKEN TO \P     PROPERLY ANCHOR BALES TO INSURE AGAINST WASHOUT.\P       \P2. FILTER FABRIC BARRIER: FILTER FABRIC BARRIERS CAN BE USED \P  
  3
 BELOW DISTURBED AREAS SUBJECT TO SHEET AND RILL EROSION\P   WITH THE FOLLOWING LIMITATIONS:\P\P  A. WHERE THE MAXIMUM SLOPE BEHIND THE BARRIER IS 33 PERCENT.\P  B. IN MINOR SWALES OR DITCH LINES WHERE THE MAXIMUM \P     CONTRIBUTING DRAINAGE AREA IS
  3
 NO GREATER THAN 2 ACRES.\P\P3. BRUSH BARRIER WITH FILTER FABRIC: BRUSH BARRIER MAY BE \P   USED BELOW DISTURBED AREAS SUBJECT TO SHEET AND RILL \P   EROSION WHERE ENOUGH RESIDUE MATERIAL IS AVAILABLE ON SITE.\P\P4. LEVEL SPREADER: A LEVEL SPREADER M
  3
AY BE USED WHERE \P   SEDIMENT FREE STORM RUNOFF IS INTERCEPTED AND DIVERTED    \P   AWAY FROM THE GRADED AREAS ONTO UNDISTURBED STABILIZED\P   AREAS. THIS PRACTICE APPLIES ONLY IN THOSE SITUATIONS \P   WHERE THE SPREADER CAN BE CONSTRUCTED ON UNDIST
  3
URBED \P   SOIL AND THE AREA BELOW THE LEVEL LIP IS STABILIZED. THE \P   WATER SHOULD NOT BE ALLOWED TO RECONCENTRATE AFTER RELEASE.\P\P5. STOCKPILING MATERIAL:  NO EXCAVATED MATERIAL SHALL BE \P   STOCKPILED IN SUCH A MANNER AS TO DIRECT RUNOFF DIRE
  3
CTLY \P   OFF THE PROJECT SITE INTO ANY ADJACENT WATER BODY OR \P   STORM WATER COLLECTION FACILITY.\P\P6. EXPOSED AREA LIMITATION: THE SURFACE AREA OF OPEN, RAW\P   ERODIBLE SOIL EXPOSED BY CLEARING OR GRUBBING OPERATIONS\P   OR EXCAVATION AND FILLI
  3
NG OPERATIONS SHALL NOT EXCEED \P   10 ACRES. THIS REQUIREMENT MAY BE WAIVED FOR LARGE \P   PROJECTS WITH AN EROSION CONTROL PLAN WHICH \P   DEMONSTRATES THAT OPENING OF ADDITIONAL AREAS WILL NOT \P   SIGNIFICANTLY AFFECT OFF-SITE DEPOSIT OF SEDIMENT
  3
S.\P\P7. INLET PROTECTION: INLETS AND CATCH BASINS WHICH  \P   DISCHARGE DIRECTLY OFF-SITE SHALL BE PROTECTED FROM SEDIMENT\P   -LADEN STORM RUNOFF UNTIL THE COMPLETION OF ALL\P   CONSTRUCTION OPERATIONS THAT MAY CONTRIBUTE SEDIMENT\P   TO THE INLET.
  3
\P\P8. TEMPORARY SEEDING: AREAS OPENED BY CONSTRUCTION \P   OPERATIONS AND THAT ARE NOT ANTICIPRED TO BE RE-\P   EXCAVATED OR DRESSED AND RECEIVE FINAL GRASSING \P   TREATMENT WITHIN 30 DAYS SHALL BE SEEDED WITH A QUICK \P   GROWING GRASS SPECIES WHI
  3
CH WILL PROVIDE AN EARLY COVER \P   DURING THE SEASON IN WHICH IT IS PLANTED AND WILL NOT \P   AFTER COMPETE WITH PERMANENT GRASSING.\P\P9. TEMPORARY SEEDING AND MULCHING: SLOPES STEEPER THAN 6:1     \P   THAT FALL WITHIN THE CATEGORY ESTABLISHED IN 
  3
PARAGRAPH  \P   8 ABOVE SHALL ADDITIONALLY RECEIVE MULCHING OF   \P   APPROXIMATELY 2 INCHES  LOOSE MEASURE OF MULCH MATERIAL \P   CUT INTO THE SOIL OF THE SEEDED AREA ADEQUATE TO PREVENT \P   MOVEMENT OF SEED AND MULCH.\P     \P10. TEMPORARY GRASSIN
  3
G: THE SEEDED OR SEEDED AND MULCHED \P    AREA(S) SHALL BE ROLLED AND WATERED OR HYDROMULCHED\P    OR OTHER SUITABLE METHODS IF REQUIRED TO ASSURE OPTIMUM \P    GROWING CONDITIONS FOR THE ESTABLISHMENT OF A GOOD \P    GRASS COVER.\P\P11. TEMPORARY RE
  3
GRASSING: IF, AFTER 14 DAYS FROM SEEDING, THE   \P    TEMPORARY GRASSED AREAS HAVE NOT ATTAINED A MINIMUM OF    \P    75 PERCENT GOOD GRASS COVER, THE AREA WILL BE REWORKED \P    AND ADDITIONAL SEED APPLIED SUFFICIENT TO ESTABLISH THE \P    DESIRED V
  3
EGETATIVE COVER.\P\P12. MAINTENANCE: ALL FEATURES OF THE PROJECT DESIGNED AND\P    CONSTRUCTED TO PREVENT EROSION AND SEDIMENT SHALL BE\P    MAINTAINED DURING THE LIFE OF THE CONSTRUCTION SO AS TO\P    FUNCTION AS THEY WERE ORIGINALLY DESIGNED AND \P
  3
    CONSTRUCTED.\P\P13. PERMANENT EROSION CONTROL: THE EROSION CONTROL \P    FACILITIES OF THE PROJECT SHOULD BE DESIGNED TO MINIMIZE \P    THE IMPACT ON THE OFF SITE FACILITIES.\P     \P14. PERMANENT SEEDING: ALL AREAS WHICH HAVE BEEN DISTURBED\P   
  1
 BY CONSTRUCTION WILL, AS A MINIMUM, BE SEEDED. THE SEEDING \P    MIX MUST PROVIDE BOTH LONG-TERM VEGETATION AND RAPID \P    GROWTH SEASONAL VEGETATION. SLOPES STEEPER THAN 4:1 \P    SHALL BE SEEDED AND MULCHED OR SODDED.
  7
